WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
Hi everyone, it's Jerry. I have a brief attacking game to share with you. This was played at

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:15.000
the 2019 St. Louis Rapid &amp; Blitz, a blitz game here between Fabiano Caruana on the white

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.200
end and Ding Liren. Those of you who are Caro-Kann players will almost certainly enjoy this one.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.920
It's not every day you get to see the Caro-Kann win in only 26 moves.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.520
Now with the setup Ding Liren goes with in this one, he takes what's normally a slower,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.340
more positional opening and turns it into an attacking weapon. So really whichever side

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.940
of the board you find yourself on, I think this is an important game to be familiar with.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:56.720
We work with an exchange, Caro-Kann. Standard moves now, Bd3, Nc6, and c3 to defend the

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:03.080
pawn and secure the bishop. One quick note to help make this game more memorable for

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:10.800
you, each side takes at least one step to prevent the active deployment of the enemy

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:22.560
queen bishop, or the bad bishops in this case. We see this on move 5 with Qc7, no Bf4, and

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:33.080
also h3, no Bg4 eventually. Of course, playing it straight away is not a good idea, but this

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:40.780
move once it's in, Bg4 becomes an idea. Now for right now, when I see h3, I view it as

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:49.960
a preventer, defending a square, and also producing a flight square for white once castled.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:56.120
As play develops, as black's setup is in place, I start to see h3 in a different way,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:08.040
in a negative way for white. We continue with Nf6, Na3, looking to maybe converge on c7,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:20.520
a quick Nb5, Bf4. This idea is ruled out altogether with a6, Nc2, and g6. At first

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:28.320
glance, you may say black's preparing to fianchetto. This is not the primary function behind g6.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:37.240
Instead, it is to develop the bad bishop. In general, black is welcome to this exchange,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.400
not the move played in the game. Black is welcome to this because of the great grip

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:49.680
now over the e4 square. This knight is really well supported on e4. Also, you could view

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.600
it, this half-open g-file is now that much more appealing for black. With the pawn now

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:04.160
on h3, g3 is that much more weakened. A knight on e4 is not easily kicked either. One day

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:11.600
if you ever play f3, the knight has a jump into g3. Caruana's play in this one is to

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:20.720
duck the bad for good bishop exchange. In comes e6, proceed with caution playing a move

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.640
like this. The bishop doesn't have any retreat squares now, so before you play this move,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.780
you have to ask yourself, am I going to be trapped if g4? You're not, but still something

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:40.860
to question. The bishop has the e4 square or the capture available. From here, castles,

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:50.180
and now h5. So this is setting up possibly a fishing pole idea. It is available, a possible

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:57.460
knight jump into g4. If captured, black has the h-file to work with, possibly a mate on

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:06.940
h2. In addition to a fishing pole idea, you could also say that black is now one step

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:19.980
closer to engaging with this h3 pawn. Just two tempi away, 1-2, g5, g4. This is now a

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:29.660
possible hook for an attack. From here, knight e1, bishop d6, a very flexible post for the

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:37.820
dark square bishop. No fianchetto, kind of biting on a rock from g7. Stares at both sides of the

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:46.900
board from d6. It fights over these two squares that the pawn supports. Knight d3, knight e4

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:54.700
brings us to the halfway point of the game. Both of these knights are focused on e5. Now that the

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:01.100
knight is on e4, if a knight hops in to this square, maybe it's going to be kicked straight

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:10.340
away with f6. Bishop e3, no same-sides castles in this game. Queenside castles on board. So

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:18.540
this is really, once queen-side castles is in, this is really where you see this h3 pawn as a

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:32.140
liability. From here, rook c1, g5 straight away. No supporting move with f6, he simply gets on

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:43.500
with g5. Now what's considered best in this position is to play c4 to justify this last

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:53.900
move. c4 never hits in this game and this ends up being a waste. So the suggestion c4, this is

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:03.580
a top-line captures on c4. And then after g4, it says hop in the center and allow this capture,

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:11.220
allow the g-file to open up. This is not at all easy for a human to play. It calls it about even

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:22.500
here. What is the direction for white? He takes the pawn. So now it's a race. Whose attack is

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.380
going to hit there first? This is already considered a winning position for black.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:40.700
f4, knight g3, rook f2, and now the knight is given a kick back to f3. The knight returns to

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:50.460
e4, targeting the rook. Rook f1, and the g pawn is pinned. So we got bishop takes h3. And now this

00:06:50.460 --> 00:07:03.540
is the focus, the g2 square. It's defended. In comes h4, king h2, and from here, battery on the

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:12.100
g-file. Queen looking to sweep in here with a check, next knocking out the bishop. g2 is defended,

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:22.540
or is it really? Black can still take on g2. Bishop takes. There's two ways to recapture. In

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:31.740
the game, white takes with the bishop. How does play continue if knight takes bishop?

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:44.860
Black can play with queen g3 check, king h1, and then h3. You see the idea here? There's a really

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:54.180
big threat. Let's say black moves the knight. That would seem like the most logical reply to h3. Well,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:08:03.060
the queen gets to go to g2. And it doesn't matter now which way the queen is captured. Let's say the

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:13.500
knight takes. Black would get to recapture, say double check, and then give checkmate. That is a

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:21.780
beautiful attacking sequence right there. In this game, it is bishop takes bishop, which allows queen

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:32.020
g3 check, king h1, queen takes bishop. Black is attacking for free. Black is up a pawn, in fact.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:41.580
Rook f3 played. Final move of this game. I wonder if you can spot it. Feel free to pause the video.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:53.700
Okay, black doesn't have to go anywhere with the queen. h3, and white resigns. If the bishop moves,

00:08:53.700 --> 00:09:01.620
it's mate right away. If the bishop doesn't move, it's going to be captured. What other options are

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:08.380
there? Rook takes pawn, you take the rook, and then take the bishop and say mate. And of course,

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:18.260
if the queen is captured, we would get that same idea of a double check, and then mate.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:27.260
What did you think of this one? Anyhow, feel free, as usual, to leave any feedback to this

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:32.380
video in the comment section below. I hope you enjoyed it, and maybe took a thing or two away.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.220
That's all for now. Take care. Bye.

